WEBVTT

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.280
What is up, everyone? Welcome back to your weekly dose

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.320
of awesomeness here at Paragliding Atlas Podcast.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.480
And as you can see, this time around, we're coming to you as a

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.840
little bit out of schedule. There's a whole reason behind

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:18.080
this, and I'm choosing the word reason instead of an excuse

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.240
because no matter how hard we tried, we simply were not able

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.680
to publish the episode on Thursday as planned.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.120
To put it simply, we store our recordings in a cloud instead of

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.880
on local disks because it's a secure mechanism and it needs a

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.120
little bit of editing before we hit the publish button and it

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.360
goes out to you guys on all the streaming platforms that you

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.120
guys listen to us on. This time around, however, when

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.080
I logged in to do that, I was met with nothing but an

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.760
unpleasant surprise. The last couple of recordings

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.440
that we had scheduled for the coming weeks were just simply

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.160
gone, nowhere to be seen. And of course, this took me

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.640
aback and I reached out to the support team and I asked them,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.960
hey guys, what's going on? This is this is some critical

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.120
data that needs to that needs to be handled well.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.840
And you know, what can we do about this?

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.239
And they said that we understand the gravity of the situation,

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:08.440
but the most of the guys who can fix this are out vacationing and

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.000
summer holidays. So the earliest we can do this

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.160
is sometime next week. This was of course a bit of a

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.280
bummer because after faltering on my release schedule for a

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.200
little bit in June, I was like, I promised myself that no matter

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.840
what happens, I will go out and talk to my audience every week

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.120
as planned and bring out all of this knowledgeable content to

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.360
you without fail. So I basically saw my own plants

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.120
being shattered. To be honest, the feeling wasn't

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.520
really good. It was overwhelming and endless

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:38.720
questions were coming to my head.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.040
Do I have to book my guests all over again?

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.600
Do I have to recreate the conversations and everything

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.280
else that comes around it? And as these emotions were

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.080
flooding in like sadness, helplessness, anxiety, and I

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.600
could just basically see things spiralling out of control,

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.040
damaging my own routine and repetition.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.240
It was just a very hard to digest feeling.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.640
And then I was like, wait a second, this feels a bit

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:07.040
familiar because this is exactly how we feel a lot of times while

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.960
flying. And by the way, I'm not talking

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.240
about paravating by any means, because nature of course throws

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.360
its own things at us which we accept regardless of how ever

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.160
prepared we are. But on the other end, when we

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.280
are going out and gliding on cross country trips or chasing

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.640
the World Cup podiums or you know, want to be in the lead

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:28.960
gaggle, those kind of situations where we have plan that needs to

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.520
be put into action. And when the action happens,

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.920
some tiny mistakes, some unexpected conditions, some

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.320
something unknown comes around and puts it off track and forces

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.280
us to rethink our strategy is something very common in flying.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.720
And I realised that we don't really resign, we don't just

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.040
accept the outcome. Instead we look for the best

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.960
possible Plan B when plan A is flying out of the window.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.560
So I was like, you know what, this is a sign.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.640
And in case those of you who have been following me for quite

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.200
a while, I think back in January earlier this year, I said that

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.880
I'm going to come out with solo episodes.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.080
But somehow, and of course, I can break blame this on

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.520
procrastination for a while that I just never got around to

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.720
getting them out to you guys. Because maybe it was a comfort

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.680
zone of having a guest in front of me and talking to you guys in

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.880
in a tandem manner where it was so much more easier to just have

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.200
a conversation with someone rather than just find the

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.560
strength and the courage to speak out on a topic by myself.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.840
So I was like, this is basically life telling me that, hey, it's

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.000
about time that you make this happen.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.440
So that is how this episode has come to existence and and the

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.320
topic of today's episode is managing the unknown when

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.240
something surprising happens. And the whole idea of bringing

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.600
out this episode was instead of picking up something that I

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.680
already planned and drafted was to understand the process that

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:05.320
goes on in our brain when an unexpected situation comes

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.040
around. Because when I was feeling all

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.400
of these emotions, it was a very weird feeling, something that I

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.840
was not comfortable with. So I was like, there must be a

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.600
way to manage these situations better.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.040
And I just went up on some reputable websites and started

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.640
doing my own research that, you know, what kind of chemicals

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.440
release, what is the brain function when such things are

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.200
happening? And to be honest, the simplest

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.520
answer to these questions are being aware of yourself.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.840
And I know being aware is a very broad word, but in simpler terms

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:43.480
it's being truthful and being being knowledgeable about what

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:46.560
your shortcomings are. And in paragliding terms, I

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.880
think being aware can be materialized by airtime.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:54.240
I know that I keep on talking about airtime answers a lot of

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.520
questions, but the point being that airtime allows us to find

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.080
the possible shortcomings which we otherwise cannot visualize or

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:07.040
otherwise won't be aware of. Because putting words into

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.480
practice is the only way to going out and finding out how

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.320
good you are or how complacent you are for that matter, or how

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.840
much do you even know about it. So I'd like to point out a quote

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.680
here which if you can remember is like a lot of times it is

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.680
said that when chaos hits the fan or like when shit hits the

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.520
fan, don't think, just do it and trust your training.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.840
I think this is used in combat situations a lot of times by

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.400
fighter pilots that when things are going crazy, just trust your

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.600
training because you have developed a lot of muscle memory

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.000
subconscious. And because over a period of

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.520
time by doing those tasks repeatedly, you have developed a

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.760
lot of muscle memory. Your subconscious has somehow

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:56.280
figured out a way how to do these actions that you wanted to

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.800
without taking a lot of thought behind it.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.560
So I'd like to point out a fact by an Austrian neurologist.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.200
He was a psychologist and a philosopher as well, who goes by

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.600
the name of Viktor Frankel. And he said something very

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:13.000
interesting, which was between stimulus and response.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:18.680
There is a space, and in that space is our power to choose our

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:22.760
response. And in our response lies our

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:25.640
growth and freedom. I'm just going to repeat this

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:30.200
one more time, a bit more slowly, and it is between the

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.800
stimuli and the response. So for example, every time there

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:39.400
is something that requires us to respond, there's a space means

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.080
there's a pause, there's a lag, there's some thinking that our

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.960
brain has to do. And in that pause, lag or

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:50.080
thinking is the power that we have within ourselves to choose

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:54.720
how we respond to that stimuli. And that choosing of our

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.480
response is where the actual growth and the freedom lies.

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.960
For example, let's say there's a asymmetric One response is to

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.280
just go the Safeway, pull a deployment handle and get down

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:10.160
to the ground as safely as possible without doing in a lot

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.720
of inputs. The other one is take control of

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.840
the wing, do the weight shift, go over the opposite brake or

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:18.040
whatever, depending upon, you know, the wing that you're

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:19.480
flying and the condition that you're in.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:25.600
So we always have a choice on how to respond and how we

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.240
respond is where we can actually make a difference in

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:33.560
understanding the behaviour and making life a whole lot better.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.760
I don't want to sound too philosophical right now, but

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.440
another way to put it across is that this whole process is

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:41.600
called metacognition. While metacognition might sound

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.840
like a fancy word, but it has a very simple meaning and that is

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.360
being aware of your thought patterns.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:53.240
So let's say every time there is a asymmetric, you have a certain

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:58.600
way of reacting and then being aware of that thought that comes

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:01.920
to your mind and then trying to decode it.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:04.680
For example, are you panicking every time you hit an asymmetric

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.880
or are you like, hey, this is just another day in the park?

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.520
Or are you just going out and taking it too lightly?

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.480
I'm pretty sure you get get the point.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:19.000
I'm saying so being aware of the thought pattern.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:22.480
For example, in my case, for seven months I stayed away from

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.440
putting out solo episodes no matter how well prepared I was.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.160
I'm not saying the reasons, but there was a constant pattern

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.600
that week after week, month after month, I somehow found

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:36.880
comfort in having a guest talk to me and then put out the

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:41.919
episodes rather than going out and doing the work that I plan

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:44.600
to do. So awareness of the patterns,

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.840
that is one of the very important things that you really

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:50.200
have to realise to get better. And then being aware of the

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:54.520
space, the choice of response where we have the option of

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:59.640
growing and finding our freedom is the key to becoming a better

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.400
pilot or a human or, you know, just growing generally.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.680
And, and when I was trying to dive deep into this, I was like,

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.320
what really happens in the brain?

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:09.800
Like, you know, what are the to do steps that happen so that we

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:13.600
can try and make them better? And I found out that there are

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:17.600
three key brain areas that are located in the front and centre

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:21.320
part of our brain, which are mostly useful for handling our

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:23.600
emotions, decisions and a little bit of social stuff.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.360
I'm not going to dive deep into it, but just to give you a

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:28.400
superficial idea of what we're talking about.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.600
We're talking about prefrontal cortex, which of course, as many

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.480
of you know, is like the boss of the brain that plans, decides

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:39.840
and controls our impulses. And then we have anterior

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.480
cingulate cortex. Sounds a bit complicated and I

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.880
don't think a lot of us have heard this before, but this is a

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:48.800
part of the brain that is basically monitoring the whole

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:52.440
ecosystem that is going on. So it it is the one who spots

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:56.160
the mistake, it is the one who manages the emotional conflicts.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:58.040
It is the one who helps us stay motivated.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:03.720
So a lot of feelings are being controlled by the anterior

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:06.480
cingulate cortex. And then there's something very

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:08.520
important here, which is amygdala.

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:15.200
Amygdala is the one which is actually putting our visuals

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.720
into action. It is like basically an alarm

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:19.200
system which exists in our brain.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:24.040
It helps us trigger the fear, it helps us process big emotions.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.400
So every time there is a asymmetric or every time we are

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.560
falling out of a thermal which has consequences, or every time

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:33.680
the lead gaggle is leaving us behind, amygdala is the first

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.080
one that raises the alarm and says that hey, about time we do

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.400
something about it. So how, how the team up is that

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:42.960
amygdala is the first one which freaks out and then the

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.000
singulate cortex, the anterior singulate cortex is the one

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.120
which is like, OK, what's going on?

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:50.760
You know, let me see, let me understand, let me make some

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.040
sense of it. And then once that sense has

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.000
been made, the prefrontal cortex is like, all right, guys, calm

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.840
down, I'm here now. Now let's try and make some

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:03.520
actual decisions which will make some smart choices and get our

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:06.200
life better. One very important thing here is

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:10.840
that brain has a neurotransmitter which floods

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:13.240
something known as new adrenaline.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:16.560
New adrenaline, it's a bit of a tongue twister.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.880
It's kind of similar to the regular adrenaline, but the

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:24.560
regular one targets I think heart and blood vessels from

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:27.920
what I read. And this one is flooding the the

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:31.840
impulses in the brain. So they kind of work similarly,

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:35.240
but I think new redolene is more responsible for sharpening the

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:38.720
focus and reaction times and gets us in the zone.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:41.960
So basically it helps us get in a state of high alert.

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:46.760
And in short, this is a real time crisis management system

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:50.360
kicking in. Now, when this happens, I think

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:52.200
all of us know that there are two ways to go.

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:56.000
The first one, of course, is to calm down while doing some

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.440
breathing exercises. A lot of us know about box

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:01.280
breathing. I think that is where you inhale

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.840
for like 4 seconds, hold it for four and then exhale for four

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:08.880
and then do it repeatedly. And then you kind of come back

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:12.360
to the normal levels where you can actually start making

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:15.920
rational decisions. The other one, which of course

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:20.880
is more applicable in flying is because there's no way in

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.480
dynamic conditions like you know, you're coring a thermal or

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:25.840
you're having an asymmetric, you can't just say that I'm going to

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:28.640
focus on breathing right now because it's action time, it's

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:30.880
go time like you actually have to do something.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:34.400
This is where it's, and especially it's valid for high

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:36.800
pace environments where there isn't a lot of time to think.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:40.800
We have something known as neural shortcuts which kick in.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:45.200
And these neural shortcuts are based on the scenarios that we

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:48.360
have rehearsed over a period of time or practiced again and

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.760
again and then gotten better at it.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.880
For example, you have done a full stall multiple times.

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:59.760
So first time glider stalls, you'd be, you'd be scared, you'd

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:01.480
be like, Oh my God, where did the gravity go?

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:03.920
You know, why don't I feel why the feeling of freefall?

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:07.920
But over a period of time when you have gone over that feeling

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:11.920
of freefall repeatedly, you have developed a way that your brain

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:16.680
knows that what kind of glider pressure will exist or how do I

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:20.520
have to handle the brakes. So all of this training that we

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:23.160
have done have built up the confidence in our brain to deal

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:26.520
with it when it happens at a subconscious level so that we

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:30.560
don't really have to do all of those breathing exercises and

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.600
get in the zone. Rather, because we have trained

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:37.400
it, we have had cognitive ability developed already in a

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:40.360
safe environment when we knew that the consequences weren't

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:42.600
too hard. We are quick to switch over to

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.120
operating something known as sensimotor brain.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.240
In simpler terms, this is also called as flow state, a state of

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:52.400
mind where we can focus on micro resets, which are replacing the

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:55.640
big mindfulness processes. And the science behind this is

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:58.560
that we are entering a tunnel vision of some sort where we are

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:03.000
choosing to direct our focus on things that really matter.

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:05.520
For example, you're going in for the final light, right?

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:08.840
Everybody around you is going on full bar as well.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:11.000
And somehow you get an asymmetric.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:14.920
You don't care if the line that you've chosen is correct or not.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:19.800
Right now, all you care is to get off the bar, get your ring

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:23.240
open again and get back on full bar as quickly as possible.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.640
So the idea here being that your brain, based on the practice

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:30.400
that you've done over a period of time, build up your muscle

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:33.320
memory, build up, your subconscious mind is focusing

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:38.680
for you to just pay attention to fixing the asymmetric and then

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:40.920
coming back to choosing the lines.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:44.880
So narrowing of attention is something that really happens.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:49.880
And this is a state of mind when the alertness is super high, the

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:53.080
dopamine is flowing in its full flow because there's motivation

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:57.120
and reward to be chased. And then the cortisol, which is

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:00.200
of course a stress hormone which causes the panic is in a very

